! Device Capabilities 2026 ~ a field scout mapped onto the house

''Register: Model-Standing.'' A scout read the house first (code and canon, never the memory index), then the field as it stood on 2026-09-11; every house claim names a file and a line, every field claim names a URL, a date, and quotes the page. A claim carrying neither names itself as inference.

''The operator's words, verbatim (quote license):''

> "Send a tasked spirit for 2026 device and browser capabilities to map onto our models here (for example, the big browser orgs all just agreed to an internal browser-only filesystem api, etc, etc)."

''Reading date:'' 2026-09-11. ''Source classes, in the order the scout trusted them:'' the WHATWG File System and Storage Living Standards (both "Last Updated 15 March 2026") · the vendors' own release notes and intent-to-ship threads (webkit.org "WebKit Features" posts for Safari 26.0 / 26.4 / 26.5 / 26.6; blink-dev intents; Mozilla dev-platform) · the two published standards-positions repos (`mozilla/standards-positions`, `WebKit/standards-positions`, read from their JSON and the closing comments on each issue) · the Interop 2026 announcement (webkit.org, hacks.mozilla.org, web.dev, all 2026-02-12) and the `web-platform-tests/interop` proposal issues · MDN Baseline banners and compatibility rows · caniuse · the WICG/W3C/whatwg repos (`whatwg/fs`, `w3c/webrtc-extensions`) · the Mudlet GitHub releases and wiki · Epic's Pixel Streaming 2 documentation and State of Unreal 2026 coverage. Third-party summaries (utsubo, corbado, lapcat, lofttools) ride only where a vendor page confirms them or where the scout names them as the secondary source they carry.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-filesystem-agreement>>

!! The filesystem agreement ~ what "the big browser orgs agreed" names

''The finding, stated plainly:'' the scout found no single 2026 joint statement signed by Apple, Google, and Mozilla. What the operator's phrase points at consists of ''one standard, three implementations, and two Baseline dates'' — and a 2026 wave of commentary that read the last of those as news.

# ''The standard.'' The WHATWG ''File System'' Living Standard (https://fs.spec.whatwg.org/, "Last Updated 15 March 2026"; abstract: "File System defines infrastructure for file systems as well as their API"). Its origin-private half — the ''bucket file system'' in the spec's own words: a handle "is in a bucket file system if the first item of its locator's path is the empty string" — reaches through `navigator.storage.getDirectory()`. Mozilla's 2022-11-10 intent (https://groups.google.com/a/mozilla.org/g/dev-platform/c/dsRxP4liTek) already named the shape: "The Origin Private File System (hereafter referred to as OPFS) API allows for file access to an origin-specific set of files," with "PRs 21 (syncAccessHandle) and 10 (move)" then waiting to merge — PR #21 merged 2022-09-29, PR #10 stays open at #180 (2026-01-04, "Add the `FileSystemFileHandle.move` method"). The September 2025 Review Draft published 2025-09-15 (`whatwg/fs#179`).
# ''The three implementations.'' MDN (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/File_System_API/Origin_private_file_system): "Baseline: Widely available … available across browsers since March 2023." Rows: `navigator.storage.getDirectory()` Chrome 86 · Firefox 111 · Safari 15.1; `FileSystemFileHandle.createSyncAccessHandle()` Chrome 90 · Firefox 111 · Safari 15.1. The page's own definition: "private to the origin of the page and not visible to the user like the regular file system … Permission prompts and security checks are not required to access files in the OPFS … a set of synchronous calls … that can be run inside web workers only so as not to block the main thread." The spec pins that last clause as IDL: `FileSystemSyncAccessHandle` carries `[Exposed=DedicatedWorker]`.
# ''The write half reached Baseline in September 2025.'' `FileSystemFileHandle.createWritable()` (https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/FileSystemFileHandle/createWritable): "Baseline 2025 · Newly available · Since September 2025, this feature works across the latest devices and browser versions." The vendor page that closed the gap: WebKit Features in Safari 26.0 (https://webkit.org/blog/17333/webkit-features-in-safari-26-0/, 2025-09-15): "There's also now support for the File System WritableStream API, enabling direct writing to files within the user's file system." So the three engines converged on the whole OPFS surface — async handles, writable streams, sync access handles in workers — between March 2023 and September 2025.
# ''The 2026 wave that read it as an agreement.'' Lapcat Software, 2026-05-28 (https://lapcatsoftware.com/articles/2026/5/5.html), quoting the spec's "private to the origin of the page and not visible to the user" and measuring Safari 26's `navigator.storage.estimate()` at "approximately 77 gigabytes"; Loft Tools, 2026-06-26 (https://lofttools.com/blog/opfs-origin-private-file-system/): "`FileSystemSyncAccessHandle` is only accessible inside dedicated Web Workers … OPFS lives in the same best-effort storage bucket as IndexedDB and the Cache API … `navigator.storage.persist()` … is a request, not a guarantee … OPFS is working storage, not delivery."

''What the agreement guarantees'' (the Storage Living Standard, https://storage.spec.whatwg.org/, 2026-03-15): "A storage bucket is a place for storage endpoints to store data"; a local bucket's mode reads `"best-effort"` until the `"persistent-storage"` permission lands, then `"persistent"`; "A user agent that comes under storage pressure should clear network state and local storage buckets whose mode is "best-effort", ideally prioritizing removal in a manner that least impacts the user"; `persist()` resolves true only when the mode becomes persistent; `estimate()` returns `usage` and `quota`; and "The storage quota of a storage shelf is an implementation-defined conservative estimate of the total amount of bytes it can hold." The vendor numbers behind "implementation-defined" (web.dev storage-for-the-web, updated 2024-09-23): "Chrome allows the browser to use up to 80% of total disk space. An origin can use up to 60% of the total disk space" · "Firefox allows the browser to use up to 50% of free disk space. An eTLD+1 group may use up to 2GB" · Safari "appears to allow about 1GB … increasing the limit in 200MB increments" — superseded on Safari 26 by the ~77 GB estimate lapcat measured and WebKit's own "each origin can store up to around 60% of total disk" that article quotes.

''What it withholds:'' a user-visible directory (by definition: "not visible to the user"); cross-origin reach (a bucket file system keys off the origin, one storage shelf per origin); persistence without the permission (best-effort until `persist()` answers true — and Firefox prompts where Chrome and Safari decide from interaction history, as the house gate already records at `packages/lararium-browser/src/secure-context-gate.ts:131-134`); a second writer on a sync handle (the spec's `createSyncAccessHandle` takes an exclusive lock on the file; WebKit's position issue #238 "Multiple Readers and Writers in File System Access API" stands at no position, Mozilla's #861 positive); and on WebKit, survival past seven days of Safari use without interaction ("deleting all of a website's script-writable storage after seven days of Safari use without user interaction on the site" — https://webkit.org/blog/10218/full-third-party-cookie-blocking-and-more/, 2020-03-24 — with the exemption "Web applications added to the home screen are not part of Safari and thus have their own counter of days of use").

''The house seat:''

* ''The browser CAS `cid/` tier ALREADY rides it.'' `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-genesis.ts:37-38` (`navigator.storage.getDirectory()` → `root.getDirectoryHandle(OPFS_CAS_DIR, { create: true })`), `:42-43` (`createWritable()` per blob, keyed by CID), `:63` `fetchGenesisCasToOpfs`, `:94-97` `readCasBlobFromOpfs`; the worker pulls "engine + plugin bytes by CID from the OPFS CAS" (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-sovereign-island-model.ts:45-47`, `resolveByCid: (cid) => readCasBlobFromOpfs(cid)`). `packages/lararium-mesh/src/cas.ts:8-10` names the pair: "the SAME two derivations here over its own platform I/O (OPFS · nodefs)". The write leg uses the async `createWritable` that reached Baseline 2025-09; the house never calls `createSyncAccessHandle` anywhere in `packages/` (grep, 2026-09-11) even though the island runs in the dedicated worker the spec exposes it to — #/moves 3.
* ''The pending shape'' (a pointer whose bytes the local `cid/` lacks — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/blob-carriage#/proposed-law">> clause 5) maps onto "best-effort" eviction cleanly: an evicted OPFS blob reads as PENDING and re-fetches by CID, never as a lost identity. `open-browser-vessel.ts:275` already names the class: "LOCAL copy is gone (IndexedDB quota eviction under storage pressure, or corruption)."
* ''The projector never reaches a folder the human sees, on any engine, without the File System Access picker'' — and two of three engines refuse that picker (#/refusals-and-floors). A browser vessel therefore ''projects to OPFS alone'' (an origin-private disk the operator cannot open in Finder or Files), and the human-visible `bags/` / `wikis/` projection of <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/disk-projection">> stays a NODE seat. The honest absence: on a phone or a Safari/Firefox desktop the "disk" the vessel writes reads as the browser's, not the operator's; a `showDirectoryPicker` projection stands as a Chromium-only ''offer'', never the vessel's floor.
* ''Per-persona planes'' — the Storage Buckets rhyme (#/pattern-integrity-rhymes) breaks on the vendor split: Chromium alone ships buckets, so the house keeps its persona planes inside ONE bucket (the IDB stores at `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-vessel-identity.ts:91-108` and the `${idbName}:repo` Automerge store at `open-browser-vessel.ts:369`), partitioned by key, never by bucket.

<<~/ahu>>

