<<~ ahu #/refusals-and-floors>>

!! Refusals and floors ~ every vendor refusal quoted, and the floor the vessel falls to

''The law that reads this slot:'' a refused capability maps onto a FLOOR the vessel falls to, never a red. The cap-stack (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier">>) self-tightens only; a vessel declares the caps it lights (`vessel-caps.mem:54`), so a refusal removes a cap from the declaration and nothing else.

# ''File System Access (a user-visible directory).'' WebKit #28, annevk 2023-02-08: "Colleagues and I have discussed this and don't see a way to grant write access to the end user's local file system in a way that safeguards the end user's interests … Allowing usage of the new APIs for read access makes sense." Mozilla #154, martinthomson 2020-08-05: "my initial assessment is that the protections described in the spec are inadequate and I am of the opinion that this should be marked *harmful*"; annevk 2021-06-17: "`getDirectory()` (formerly `getOriginPrivateDirectory()`) has identical properties to Indexed DB and is therefore fine." Mozilla #738 (a subset), 2023-01-24: "we've concluded that we need to defer this question … we try to avoid taking specifications à la carte." ''Floor:'' the browser vessel projects to OPFS alone; the human-visible `bags/` projection stays with the node; a Chromium picker stands as an offer the operator may light, never a dependency.
# ''Isolated Web Apps and Direct Sockets.'' Mozilla #799, martinthomson 2024-09-10: "The way that the Web has been most successful in delivering new capabilities is not by removing the safeguards, but by finding safe ways to achieve goals … if that means an end to direct use of TCP sockets, such that the server end has to adapt, that's something we've been willing to accept"; 2025-05-08: E2EE "need[s] things like secure multiparty key exchange with MLS and a suite of integrity and transparency features, not elevated privileges." WebKit #184, 2023-10-04: "It seems this depends on Web Bundles which I don't think WebKit has taken a position on" — position ''blocked''. Chrome itself: "installation can only be triggered by enterprise policy on managed devices"; from Chrome 143 "only Isolated Web Apps (IWAs) that are on a Google-managed allowlist can be installed." ''Floor:'' the relay (the Herm) carries every socket the browser cannot open; the house already refuses a roster (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/nexus-topology">>) and an allowlist reads as one.
# ''Web Bluetooth / WebUSB / Web Serial / WebHID.'' WebKit #570, annevk 2025-12-02: "The low-level nature of this API means that it is insecure, has a massive privacy risk, and perhaps most importantly doesn't meet the web platform's device-independence bar." Mozilla #95 negative, #100 negative, #459 negative; Web Serial moved from negative to neutral and ships in Firefox Nightly 151 behind a flag ("Firefox Nightly finally embraces the Web Serial API, six years after rejecting it", korben.info, 2026). ''Floor:'' no phone or tablet vessel promises a peripheral; a node vessel reaches hardware through its own process.
# ''Background Sync / Periodic Background Sync / Background Fetch.'' Mozilla #173, annevk 2019-11-20: "absent finding one this is considered `harmful`"; #214 negative. WebKit #14 concerns "privacy, power"; #149 "privacy, maintenance". ''Floor:'' a browser leaf syncs while open; the waking floor belongs to a node that never sleeps (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor">>).
# ''Web Push on iOS.'' Apple grants the Push API to Home Screen web apps only (vendor docs above, unchanged through iOS 26). ''Floor:'' presence on a phone rides the open relay socket; a push arrives only after the household installs — which the WebKit seven-day exemption rewards twice.
# ''Device Memory / Compute Pressure / Battery.'' WebKit #645 oppose (closed 2026-04-14), #255 oppose ("privacy, device independence"); Firefox removed Battery in 2016; Safari never shipped it. ''Floor:'' a vessel reads its own capacity off the caps it declares, never off a probe the platform withholds — the DPI ceiling gate at `sensorium-machina.mem:103` already refuses a read the plane cannot carry.
# ''Storage Buckets / WebSocketStream / WebNN / FileSystemObserver.'' No refusal — silence (WebKit #181, #315, #486, #291 "none"; Mozilla positive on buckets, WebNN, FileSystemObserver). ''Floor:'' one bucket, one WebSocket, no on-device model, the house's own CAS write event.
# ''Seven days on WebKit.'' The 2020-03-24 rule stands unrevised in 2026: "deleting all of a website's script-writable storage after seven days of Safari use without user interaction on the site." ''Floor:'' the browser vessel reads its own eviction class at `secure-context-gate.ts:139-160` and reports "WebKit clears it after seven days without interaction. Installing to the home screen exempts it from that clock" — a fact, never an error.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/device-seats>>

!! Device seats ~ what each stands as under the 2026 field

The house names four standings a device may hold: a ''full vessel'' (islands, CAS, keys, an inbound gate), a ''leaf'' ("a laptop browser, a phone, a tablet. Each stands as a LEAF, CONTRACTED" — <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook">>, `:1123`; "BIND BY EDGE, NEVER BY SEED", `first-realm-arc.mem:61`), a ''herm'' (the relay that carries and never reads), and a ''probe'' (a surface that reads a seat it never holds). One capability decides each.

| seat | stands as | the deciding capability | what it can honestly promise (2026-09-11) |
|---|---|---|---|
| ''phone — iOS 26'' | a ''leaf'', contracted; a full vessel only after Home Screen install | the secure context (`secure-context-gate.ts:5-7`: "A family phone reaching a home node at `http://192.168.1.x` gets neither") → the DNS-01 wildcard path (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context#/why-dns-01">>) | OPFS + Ed25519 + module workers + WebGPU + WebTransport (26.4) + PRF (26.4); NO push, NO durable storage, NO SharedWorker lifetime guarantees until installed ("every website added to the Home Screen opens as a web app", 2025-09-15); NO peripherals ever |
| ''phone — Android 16 (Chrome 149)'' | a ''leaf''; a full vessel as a WebAPK | the same secure-context wall; SharedWorker returned in 148 (2026-05) | everything iOS grants plus Web Push without install, Storage Buckets, Web Serial (148), SharedWorker; Firefox-on-Android lacks WebCodecs and WebGPU |
| ''tablet'' | a ''leaf'' that may stand a household's ''waking floor'' ("a tablet left on a shelf … becomes the household's sync-node the day somebody needs one", `waking-floor.mem:148`) | Home Screen install (the seven-day exemption) + `persist()` | the phone's promises with a screen the canvas field names as the retreat from the phone (`canvas-field-scout.mem:365`); never an inbound socket — "a browser cannot listen on a socket" (`open-browser-vessel.ts:9-10`) |
| ''desktop browser'' | a ''leaf'' on every engine; a ''full vessel minus the inbound gate'' | `crossOriginIsolated` unused; Web Locks (Baseline 2022) decides one holder per origin | OPFS sync handles in the island worker (all three engines) · Web Locks · WebGPU (Safari 26+, Firefox Windows/macOS) · WebTransport · PRF; a user-visible folder on Chromium alone |
| ''node'' | the ''full vessel'' and the ''herm'' | the inbound gate: UDS `/tmp/lares-<uid>/<digest>.sock` (`packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel.ts:56,171-173`), the WS relay + `DaemonAuthGate` (`open-node-vessel.ts:360-362`), Socket B (`:567-572`), `worker_threads` islands (`worker-handle.ts:6,28`), the nodefs Repo (`open-node-vessel.ts:26,360`) | the human-visible `bags/` / `wikis/` projection; hardware; the waking floor; the only seat that may hold a reach credential ("A browser vessel holds NO reach credential … a node in the fleet holds", `blob-carriage.mem:172`) |
| ''Mudlet 5.0'' | a ''probe'' and a ''leaf behind a node'' — never a vessel | transport: telnet only ("Mudlet does not yet support Websockets", wiki 2020-03-27, unchanged in the 5.0 notes); HTTPS verbs (`getHTTP` … `customHTTP`, `downloadFile`) outbound only | a Lua object-model guest (`wiki-layer-ontology.mem:249`) that reads a projection over HTTP from a node's bulb read-face (`tiddler-carriage.mem:80`) and writes through a node-held verb door; 5.0's keychain-held reconnect token rhymes with a device key it never mints itself |
| ''Unreal 5.6–5.8 / UE6'' | a ''guest island over a host bridge''; with Pixel Streaming 2, a ''camera'' whose vessel sits on the streaming server | Pixel Streaming 2's internal WebRTC layer (UE 5.5+) — the browser sees a video + a data channel, the vessel runs server-side | the ECS mapping "slot-for-slot" (`vessel-caps.mem:62`); a Verse/SceneGraph guest at UE6 (Early Access "end of 2027"); until then the C++/Blueprint 5.x host with WebSocket/HTTP plugins the scout did not read (inference, unverified) |

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/pattern-integrity-rhymes>>

!! Pattern-integrity rhymes ~ where a 2026 capability IS a house law under another name, and where it breaks

# ''Web Locks IS the single-owner law.'' "There can be only one 'exclusive' holder of a lock" (MDN) ↔ "one activation runs per grain at a time (single-owner preserved)" (`vessel-island-pool-core.ts:244`), "One physical palace, one spelling, one holder" (`palace-path.ts:59`). ''Where it breaks:'' `steal` — "any held locks with the same name will be released, and the request will be granted, preempting any queued requests" — hands a second holder the grain by force; the house law folds concurrent opens into ONE and never preempts (`:148`). Ride the lock, forbid `steal`.
# ''OPFS sync access handles ARE the island's private disk.'' `[Exposed=DedicatedWorker]` ↔ "Every hot island runs via runBrowserSovereignWorker" (`browser-sovereign-island-model.ts:20`); the nodefs face "the nodefs face of the origin-shared OPFS CAS" (`node-cas.ts:6-9`). ''Where it breaks:'' "origin-shared" — every island in one origin shares one bucket file system, so the disk reads as the ORIGIN's, never the island's; the sovereignty line "MAY share machine/disk/relay, MUST NOT share replica/heap/keys" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/lararium-canonical-model">>) holds because the CAS carries content-addressed, immutable bytes — a shared disk of hashes leaks nothing a shared replica would.
# ''Passkey PRF IS a device-minted vessel key.'' "credential-bound cryptographic secrets" (Safari 26.4) ↔ "the dyad veil derives per-handle from the DEVICE-MINTED vessel-key, NEVER the seed" (the veil ruling, <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/secure-context#/two-key-classes">>). ''Where it breaks — twice:'' (a) a SYNCED passkey (iCloud Keychain, Google Password Manager) returns "identical" PRF output on every device the vendor syncs to — which makes it a SEED-class secret wearing a device-class name; (b) "Treat PRF as an enhancement, not a dependency" — Firefox on Android lacks it, iOS withholds extension data from roaming keys. So PRF may WRAP the persona-root seed at rest (unwrapping needs presence), replacing the cleartext-on-disk finding at `secure-context.mem:127` with a presence-gated one — and never mints the vessel key itself.
# ''Storage Buckets ARE per-persona planes.'' "evict the grouped data independently" ↔ the persona planes that "Register every plane, MOUNT one" (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/reach-plane">>). ''Where it breaks:'' Chromium alone; and a bucket shares the origin's shelf, so eviction independence never reaches sovereignty — the planes stay key-partitioned inside one bucket on every engine.
# ''WebTransport datagrams ARE the carriage lane.'' "multiple streams that won't block each other" + unreliable datagrams ↔ Socket B "SEPARATE from the Automerge /ws relay (Socket A)" (`open-node-vessel.ts:567-572`), ciphertext blocks a relay serves "secret-free" (`cas-transit.ts:17`). ''Where it breaks:'' the herm sits on a node, and Node ships no WebTransport server in core; the lane rides HTTP/3 the home hearth (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/nexus-topology">>) must terminate with a certificate — the same DNS-01 wall, one layer up.
# ''A Service Worker IS a herm at the waking floor.'' It carries fetches and pushes for an origin while no page stands ↔ "the 'dumb' relay IS the Herm, LOAD-BEARING". ''Where it breaks:'' a Service Worker holds no socket and no timer the vendor honours (Background Sync refused on two engines; Push on iOS only after install); it wakes for a push the relay sends — so it rhymes with the herm's DOORBELL, never the herm.
# ''`persist()` IS the residency verb PIN.'' "cannot be cleared without user consent" ↔ clause 7 of the blob law, "GC and PIN ride the residency verbs and the cap-tier — DESIGNED, UNBUILT" (`blob-carriage.mem:190`). ''Where it breaks:'' `persist()` pins the ORIGIN whole ("all-or-nothing per origin", `secure-context-gate.ts:134`) where PIN pins a CID; the house grain stays finer than the platform's.
# ''iOS "every site can be a web app" IS the founding rite's "stand".'' A Home Screen install turns a tab into a seat with its own day-counter ↔ found · stand · seed (<<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/founding-runbook">>). ''Where it breaks:'' the install belongs to the human's gesture, never to the vessel; a vessel may ASK (the `beforeinstallprompt` Chromium alone fires) and on WebKit may only explain.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/moves>>

!! Moves ~ the five smallest changes the field invites, ranked by what inherits shape

Ranked substrate → face → gatherings (GROUNDS BEFORE WINS); none enacted here.

# ''Read the ceiling: `navigator.storage.estimate()` beside `requestDurableStorage`'' — `packages/lararium-browser/src/secure-context-gate.ts:139-160`. The gate reads the eviction CLASS and never the QUOTA; one call adds `usage`/`quota` to `StorageReading` so the vessel names how many CAS blobs fit before best-effort eviction fires (Safari 26 answers ~77 GB, Chrome 60 % of disk, Firefox 2 GB per eTLD+1). ''Inherits:'' substrate; every browser leaf; the PENDING shape gains a number. ''Witness first:'' a StorageHost with `estimate` absent (Safari < 26 / a private window) must read `"unknown"`, never throw.
# ''Light the single-owner law in the browser: `navigator.locks.request("lares:vessel:<idbName>", { ifAvailable: true }, …)` around the vessel open'' — `packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts:321` (`idbName = "lares:vessel"`) before `:369` (`new IndexedDBStorageAdapter(…)`). A second tab on the same origin today opens a second holder over one IDB store and one OPFS CAS — the collision `face-join.ts:13` names for keyhive ("a second holder against a single-owner store"). `ifAvailable` refuses loud and explains (the `assertCanMint` register, `:103`); `steal` stays forbidden by the rhyme. ''Inherits:'' substrate; Baseline 2022 on all three engines, in workers too. ''Witness first:'' two `openBrowserVessel` calls in one page — the second must refuse with the holder named.
# ''Read the OPFS CAS with `createSyncAccessHandle` inside the island'' — `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-sovereign-island-model.ts:45-47` (`resolveByCid: (cid) => readCasBlobFromOpfs(cid)`) and `browser-genesis.ts:94-97`. The spec exposes the sync handle to exactly the dedicated worker the island runs in; a `read(buffer)` on an immutable content-addressed blob needs no lock dance, and the main thread never enters the path. ''Inherits:'' substrate/face; the breath path on every engine (Chrome 102 · Safari 16.4 · Firefox 111). ''Witness first:'' `readCasBlobFromOpfs` keeps its async face; the sync leg rides behind it and a missing handle falls to the async read.
# ''Offer the install, never require it'' — `packages/lararium-app/src/main.ts:12` names the seven-day clock and "a home-screen install" and then ships no manifest. A `manifest.webmanifest` (`display: standalone`, icons) plus a one-line explanation on WebKit ("install to keep this vessel past seven days") lets the phone leaf earn the exemption and, on iOS, the Push API. ''Inherits:'' face; the phone/tablet seats; the presence floor. ''Witness first:'' the explanation renders on `isSecureContext === true && !navigator.standalone`, never on a node or a desktop.
# ''Hold one vessel per origin across tabs: a SharedWorker holder'' — `packages/lararium-browser/src/worker-handle.ts:39` (`new Worker(url, { type: "module" })`). With Chrome 148 returning SharedWorker to Android (2026-05) and Interop-2027 proposal #1382 on `extendedLifetime`, the daemon island could stand ONCE per origin and hand each tab a MessagePort — move 2's lock becomes the door, this becomes the house behind it. ''Inherits:'' gatherings; held until move 2 stands and Firefox/Safari lifetime semantics get measured (Safari 16+ ships SharedWorker; its lifetime under the seven-day rule stays unread). ''Witness first:'' two tabs, one island, one keyhive provider — the `face-join.ts:13` collision closes.

''What to refuse'' — a capability that would fuse a gate the house keeps apart:

* ''Isolated Web Apps + Direct Sockets'' — a browser opening TCP/UDP itself fuses the sovereign plane with the reach plane ("KYC lives at the web2 bridge"; the bridge sits behind an island boundary, `field-collision.mem:116`), and a Google-managed allowlist stands a roster the attestation-seat ruling refuses. Two engines refuse it; the relay already carries the socket.
* ''Passkey PRF as the SEED'' — a synced passkey syncs the secret through the vendor; the veil ruling names the device-minted key and never the seed. PRF wraps; it never mints.
* ''Storage Buckets as sovereignty'' — a bucket partitions eviction, never keys; "share substrate, not sovereignty" reads the line.
* ''`SharedArrayBuffer` + `Atomics.wait` on the shore'' — a synchronous DOM shim buys `preventDefault`-after-the-fact and the off-main-thread law refuses it: "DECLARE AT BINDING, NEVER DECIDE AT HANDLING" (`dreamdom.mem:103`).
* ''File System Access as the browser projector'' — a Chromium-only door standing where two engines refuse makes the leaf's floor engine-shaped; keep it an offer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/questions>>

!! Questions ~ only the operator's

# Does the phone leaf ''require'' the Home Screen install to stand (the seven-day exemption + Push as the price of a seat), or does the vessel stand uninstalled and merely ''explain'' what a tab's storage is worth?
# Does a browser vessel ever want the human-visible folder (Chromium's `showDirectoryPicker`, persisted permission) as a lit cap on the desktop seat — or does the projection to a folder stay the node's alone, everywhere, so the leaf's floor reads the same on all three engines?
# Does PRF-wrapped custody ''replace'' the cleartext-on-disk seed (`secure-context.mem:127`) as the persona-root's resting form, or sit beside it as an opt-in — and does a synced passkey (iCloud/Google) count as a device or as a cloud in the veil ruling?
# Which seat does Mudlet hold in the fleet: a probe over a node's bulb read-face (telnet-only, HTTPS-out), or a leaf with a verb door — and does the house build the WebSocket⟷telnet herm Mudlet's wiki names, or wait on Mudlet?
# Unreal: build the guest island against 5.6–5.8 (C++ host, Pixel Streaming 2 camera) now, or hold for UE6 Verse/SceneGraph at "end of 2027"?

<<~/ahu>>

